nt (exchange transfusion) is possible only in limited situations, such as severe jaundice in newborns or sickle cell crisis. In adults, it’s extremely risky: massive immune reactions, electrolyte imbalances, clotting problems, and the sheer volume of donor blood needed makes it impractical as a cure for chronic diseases. ### 5. **The “clean blood” would quickly become infected again** - Even if you could somehow flush out all circulating virus or cancer cells, the remaining reservoir would immediately begin releasing new copies into the fresh blood, essentially nullifying the procedure. In short, blood is just the transport medium, not the source of these diseases. A cure requires targeting the root—latent HIV in tissues or the malignant cells in bone marrow/solid organs—not just swapping out what’s in the veins.
